# Contributing

How {{ project_name }} is built and how to change it. Using it: [README.md](README.md). Conventions that apply to
every project, with their reasons: [GUIDE.md]({{ guide_url }}).

## Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    cli["cli/<br/>one module per console script"]:::side
    config["config.py<br/>entry config schemas"]:::pure
    subgraph core["core/"]
        schema["config, configgen, registry"]:::pure
        effects["runs, logging, seeding, paths"]:::side
    end
    cli --> config --> schema
    cli --> effects
    classDef pure fill:#d9f2ec,stroke:#1b7f6b,color:#0b3d33
    classDef side fill:#f8e3d8,stroke:#b4532a,color:#4a1f0c
```

Teal boxes decide (pure: same input, same output); rust boxes act (files, processes, network). Each seam keeps the two
apart, so a dry run can execute the pure half for real.

Dependencies point down: `cli` > `config` > `core`. The import-linter contract in `pyproject.toml` enforces the
order and is exhaustive, so a new subpackage fails `make check` until it is placed in `layers`.

## Repository layout

```text
configs/                  Hydra YAML; generated files say so in their first line
  main.yaml               entry config of {{ cli }}-run (generated from MainConfig)
  hydra/default.yaml      Hydra writes nothing; the app writes runs/
src/{{ package }}/
  cli/                    one module per console script: the composition roots
  config.py               entry config schemas + GENERATED (files `make configs` writes)
  core/                   building blocks: config, configgen, registry, errors, logging, paths, runs, seeding
tests/                    mirrors src/ module by module
  test_invariants.py      configs compose and are fresh, kinds build, import layers hold
  integration/            end to end, `-m integration`
notebooks/                exploration only, outputs stripped; reference/ for third-party notebooks
runs/ data/               gitignored
```

## Families

A family is a concept with several implementations chosen by a config `kind`:

```text
src/{{ package }}/<family>/
  base.py        <Family> (attrs ABC), <Family>Config, <Family>Factory (config_group = "<family>")
  items/
    __init__.py  import_submodules(__name__)
    <kind>.py    @<Family>Factory.register("<kind>") above @define
```

```python
class SquareConfig(ShapeConfig):
    """Square settings."""

    side_m: float = Field(default=1.0, gt=0, description="side length")


@ShapeFactory.register("square")  # Outermost: attrs replaces the class.
@define
class Square(Shape[SquareConfig]):  # The type argument binds Square.Config.
    """Axis-aligned square."""

    def area(self) -> float:
        return self.config.side_m**2
```

`ShapeFactory.from_config({"kind": "square", "side_m": 2})` validates against `SquareConfig` and builds the item;
`make configs` writes `configs/shape/kinds/square.yaml`. The family's `__init__.py` imports `items`, so importing
the family registers its kinds; `make configs` and the invariant tests import the whole package, so they see every
family even before any code uses it.

## Extending

| to add | create | and |
|---|---|---|
| a family | `src/{{ package }}/<family>/base.py` + `items/` | place it in the import-linter `layers`; `make configs` |
| an item | `src/{{ package }}/<family>/items/<kind>.py` | `make configs` |
| an entry config | a schema in `config.py`, listed in `GENERATED` | a `cli/<command>.py` and a console script |
| a setting | a `Field(default=..., description=...)` on the schema | `make configs` |

## Tests

```bash
make test               # unit tests, a few seconds
make test-integration   # end to end, behind the integration marker
```

Fakes, not mocks (`unittest.mock` is banned by ruff). Each case covers a distinct behaviour; child processes and
servers only for what cannot be measured in process.

## Commits

Conventional Commits with the standard types (`feat fix docs refactor test build ci perf style chore revert`), an
optional scope, a lowercase imperative subject. Short-lived `<type>/<slug>` branches, squash-merged. The commit-msg
hook checks the format.
